   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n(n+1)} \\). Take f(x) to be the series' general term with n replaced by x, and the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Ln",
         2
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            1,
            [
               "Multiply",
               "x",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  1
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = \\ln{\\left(\\frac{x}{x + 1} \\right)} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{1}{x(x+1)} \\, dx = \\ln{\\left(\\frac{b}{b + 1} \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Ln",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  "b",
                  [
                     "Add",
                     "b",
                     1
                  ]
               ]
            ],
            [
               "Ln",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Ln",
            2
         ]
      }
   ],
